tools agent_message send --to agent-01M2K1RFG71SJETW3996TFKAT0 --attachments /downloads/LitePDF-Setup.exe --message-stdin <<'EOF'
Rebuilt installer attached (20.5 MB, litepdf.exe inside 15.1 MB). Everything from the last build plus the cursor fix.

CURSOR (item 12): normal arrow by default. The text I-beam now shows only in Text mode, or in Select/Highlight/Underline/Strike when the mouse is actually over a character. Pen/Redact/Sign show a crosshair, Eraser a hand. Built and runs here; the hover behaviour itself is untested (Wine can't open the window), so only his PC can confirm.

OCR CONFIGURATION (his question). Answer: no configuration needed. Read from the source and the installed folder (and the installer was run under Wine):
- The app looks for ocr\tesseract.exe and ocr\tessdata next to litepdf.exe. The installer puts both there (tesseract.exe 5.7 MB, English "best" data 15 MB, so English is bundled). Only if the bundled exe is missing does it fall back to a "tesseract" on PATH. The portable litepdf.exe alone, copied elsewhere without the ocr folder, cannot OCR.
- No VC++ runtime or extra DLL: tesseract.exe and litepdf.exe import only Windows system DLLs and the Windows 10+ universal C runtime (api-ms-win-crt-*, built into Windows 10/11). Read from the file's import strings, not run on real Windows. A very old Windows (7/8) without that runtime would fail to launch it.
- Would a missing piece give "no words, no error"? In the build he ran, a failed launch or missing data returned an error that WAS shown ("OCR failed: ..."), so the silent result suggests the engine launched and exited normally with nothing. One real display bug in that build: the "OCR found N words" message was immediately overwritten by "Done (unsaved...)", so he could not see a 0 count, and the document was reloaded even for 0 words. Fixed in the last build (now: explicit "found no words", log in %APPDATA%\LitePDF\ocr.log, retries in two other layout modes).
- 0.9 s/page: a real tesseract run on a text page took 2.8-5 s per page here (Wine, 2 cores), so 0.9 s fits an engine that quickly returns empty (or pages with little to read) better than full recognition. A failed launch itself would stop the whole run on page 1 with an error, not run 5 minutes. Also possible: his pages already hold live text, so the invisible OCR layer would add nothing visible. I still cannot tell which; the ocr.log from his machine would settle it.
EOF
